\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The discriminant character of an integer}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The [quadratic center character](fdip-0049) attaches to a square
class its primitive Dirichlet character. A partition function produces
Legendre symbols of one integer. This note identifies the two on odd
primes.}

\p{Let #{d} be a nonzero integer. If #{d} is a square, the center note
uses the trivial character of conductor one. If #{d} is not a square,
let #{d_0} be its squarefree integer representative, with the sign of
#{d}, and set #{D=d_0} when #{d_0\equiv 1\pmod 4} and #{D=4d_0}
otherwise. Write #{\chi_D} for the primitive character of that
discriminant in the center note. For an odd prime #{p} that does not
divide #{d},
#{\bigl(\frac{d}{p}\bigr)=\chi_D(p)}.}
\proof{
\p{For an odd prime #{p} and an integer #{a} not divisible by #{p},
the symbol #{\bigl(\frac{a}{p}\bigr)} is #{1} when #{a} is a nonzero
square modulo #{p}, and #{-1} otherwise. The group
#{\mathbb F_p^\times} has #{(p-1)/2} squares. The equation
#{x^2=1} factors as #{(x-1)(x+1)=0}, so the kernel of the squaring map
is the two units #{1} and #{-1}. In that field, every square
#{a=b^2} satisfies #{a^{(p-1)/2}=b^{p-1}=1}. The polynomial
#{X^{(p-1)/2}-1} has at most #{(p-1)/2} roots, so the squares are all
of its roots. A nonsquare is not among them. Fermat's identity
#{a^{p-1}-1=0} factors as
#{(a^{(p-1)/2}-1)(a^{(p-1)/2}+1)=0}, so a nonsquare satisfies
#{a^{(p-1)/2}=-1} in the field. Thus there is a unique residue
#{\varepsilon} among #{1} and #{-1} such that
#{a^{(p-1)/2}\equiv\varepsilon\pmod p}, and #{\varepsilon} is the
symbol just defined. The integer power itself need not lie in that
pair.}

\p{The symbol is multiplicative. If #{p} divides neither #{a} nor
#{b}, the field powers satisfy
##{(ab)^{(p-1)/2}\equiv a^{(p-1)/2}b^{(p-1)/2}\pmod p.}
Each of those three powers is congruent to its symbol, so
##{\bigl(\frac{ab}{p}\bigr)\equiv\bigl(\frac{a}{p}\bigr)\bigl(\frac{b}{p}\bigr)\pmod p.}
Both sides lie among #{1} and #{-1}. Those two residues are distinct
modulo an odd prime, so the two sides are equal as integers. A square
#{c^2} not divisible by #{p} therefore has symbol #{1}.}

\p{Every nonzero integer has a unique squarefree kernel. Factor
#{d=\varepsilon\prod_pp^{a_p}}, with #{\varepsilon} the sign of #{d}
and each #{a_p\ge 0}. Let #{d_0} be #{\varepsilon} times the product
of the primes whose exponent is odd, and let #{f^2} absorb every even
part of an exponent. Then #{d=f^2d_0}. No square #{p^2} divides
#{d_0}. The sign is unique, and so is the set of primes of odd
exponent, so #{d_0} is unique.}

\p{The odd prime #{p} does not divide #{d}, so it divides neither
#{f} nor #{d_0}. Multiplicativity gives
#{\bigl(\frac{d}{p}\bigr)=\bigl(\frac{d_0}{p}\bigr)}. The center
note writes #{\chi_D(n)=\bigl(\frac{D}{n}\bigr)} under its Kronecker
convention, and that value is #{0} when #{p} divides #{D}. On an odd
prime that does not divide #{D}, this note reads
#{\bigl(\frac{D}{p}\bigr)} as the Legendre symbol just defined. The
prime #{2} is not given that reading.
If #{d} is a square, that note uses the trivial character, and the
symbol just proved is #{1}. If #{d} is not a square and
#{d_0\equiv 1\pmod 4}, then #{D=d_0}. The prime #{p} does not divide
#{D}, so #{\chi_D(p)=\bigl(\frac{d_0}{p}\bigr)}. If instead
#{D=4d_0}, the same prime is odd and does not divide #{d_0}, so it
does not divide #{D}. Then
#{\bigl(\frac{D}{p}\bigr)=\bigl(\frac{4}{p}\bigr)\bigl(\frac{d_0}{p}\bigr)},
and #{4} is a square, so the first factor is #{1}. In every case
#{\bigl(\frac{d}{p}\bigr)=\chi_D(p)}.}
}

\p{The center note records that #{\chi_D} is primitive of conductor
#{|D|}. This section uses that conductor and does not re-prove it.
The identification is the equality of symbols on odd primes. It is not
the law that exchanges the two arguments of a Legendre symbol.}
